## 8. Pandas against SQL

The same four tasks were solved in Pandas (from Parquet) and in SQL (SQLite); the benchmark aborts unless both give the same answer. Times are the median of ${sql_bench_repeats} runs with warm caches on one machine.

${pandas_sql_table}

${pandas_sql_verdict} SQL is not chosen for speed here: it gives declarative queries, enforced integrity, indexes, and the possibility to join the model predictions of the ML module back to transactions without loading everything into memory.

## 9. Verification and reproducibility

- **SQL is cross-checked against Pandas.** ${n_crosschecks} queries (${crosscheck_list}) are recomputed independently in Pandas from the Parquet file and compared; the pipeline stops if one differs (`src/db/crosscheck.py`). For example the duplicate count found in SQL equals the count found by the audit on the raw CSV.
- **Tests:** `python -m pytest tests/data_engineering` (synthetic data for unit tests, plus integration checks on the real dataset when it has been built).
- **One command rebuilds everything:** `python -m src.data.cli run-all`; the console menu is `python -m src.data.cli`. The split and all statistics are deterministic; the only random choice (a plot sample) uses a fixed seed.
- **Versions** of the libraries are pinned in `requirements-data.txt`.

## 10. Limitations

- The file was downloaded from a public mirror, not from Kaggle; its SHA-256 is recorded so any other copy can be compared, and the row and fraud counts match the official description.
- Validation and test contain few frauds (${val_fraud} and ${test_fraud}); the chronological split is the correct protocol but gives noisy estimates.
- The V features are anonymised, so EDA can show *that* features separate the classes but not *why*.
- The data has no absolute clock time; time-of-day statements are not possible.
- Benchmarks were measured on one machine (${bench_platform}) in warm-cache conditions; read them as relative comparisons.
- Duplicate rows and outliers are reported and kept, not removed; the ML module decides how to treat them.

